  {
    "episode_id": 74039,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74039.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "District 6 Republican, Great State of Virginia, it's a Ben Klein, go follow him everywhere. I want to just ask a very general question if I can, because I don't know how this happened. Why exactly is it that we stopped worrying about a budget? We keep calling it a debt ceiling or a debt limit. It's really not, because you guys can change it whenever you feel like it. Why are we in a place to where I as an American citizen could never be there? My bank would call me and say you can't buy that because you have no more money and your budget doesn't balance. I can't really run a negative balance on my checkbook, maybe on paper for a day, but that's it. I'd be in big trouble. Why is it and maybe better question is when is it that the government decided we'll just spend whatever we want and just take it wherever we can get it?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The term 'debt ceiling' is misleading; it's actually a debt limit that can be changed at will.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "The U.S. government continues to spend without regard to budget constraints.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Individual citizens cannot maintain a negative balance in their checkbooks indefinitely without consequences.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "American citizens face immediate financial penalties when they exceed their available funds.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The government operates under different fiscal rules than individual citizens.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "There was a point in time when the government began spending freely without concern for funding sources.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      },
      {
        "text": "Ben Klein is a candidate associated with District 6 Republican in Virginia.",
        "confidence": 0.8
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Citizenship entails personal responsibility for financial management.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Government fiscal behavior should mirror individual household budgeting principles.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Public trust in government depends on responsible fiscal stewardship.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The public expects political leaders to uphold basic economic accountability.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Changing the debt limit reflects a lack of genuine fiscal discipline by Congress.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes that current fiscal policy undermines national economic stability.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      },
      {
        "text": "Audiences recognize the inconsistency between personal and governmental finance norms.",
        "confidence": 0.83
      },
      {
        "text": "Elected officials prioritize short-term political gains over long-term fiscal sustainability.",
